1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,860
Hello everyone.

2
00:00:03,860 --> 00:00:10,360
Today we're going to look at another natural food product and that's maple syrup.

3
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:12,850
What is this exactly?

4
00:00:12,850 --> 00:00:19,608
Well, maple syrup looks rather like clear honey, but it's not made by bees;

5
00:00:19,608 --> 00:00:22,762
it's produced from the plant fluid -

6
00:00:22,762 --> 00:00:29,520
or sap - inside the maple tree and that makes maple syrup a very natural product.

7
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:31,860
Maple syrup is a thick

8
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:36,619
golden, sweet-tasting liquid that can be bought in bottles or jars

9
00:00:36,619 --> 00:00:40,300
and poured onto food such as waffles

10
00:00:40,300 --> 00:00:45,050
and ice cream or used in the baking of cakes and pastries.

11
00:00:45,050 --> 00:00:49,200
It contains no preservatives or added ingredients

12
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:53,700
and it provides a healthy alternative to refined sugar.

13
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:57,960
Let's just talk a bit about the maple tree itself

14
00:00:58,220 --> 00:01:00,960
which is where maple syrup comes from.

15
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:04,381
So, there are many species of maple tree,

16
00:01:04,381 --> 00:01:08,820
and they'll grow without fertilizer in areas

17
00:01:08,820 --> 00:01:11,650
where there's plenty of moisture in the soil.

18
00:01:11,650 --> 00:01:16,594
However, they'll only do this if another important criterion is fulfilled,

19
00:01:16,594 --> 00:01:24,010
which is that they must have full or partial sun exposure during the day and very cool nights -

20
00:01:24,010 --> 00:01:28,130
and I'll talk more about that in a minute.

21
00:01:28,130 --> 00:01:35,015
There are only certain parts of the world that provide all these conditions: one is Canada,

22
00:01:35,015 --> 00:01:38,920
and by that, I mean all parts of Canada

23
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:43,190
and the other is the north-eastern states of North America.

24
00:01:43,190 --> 00:01:49,230
In these areas, the climate suits the trees perfectly.

25
00:01:49,230 --> 00:01:54,769
In fact, Canada produces over two-thirds of the world's maple syrup,

26
00:01:54,769 --> 00:01:59,360
which is why the five-pointed maple leaf is a Canadian symbol

27
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:03,540
and has featured on the flag since 1964.

28
00:02:03,540 --> 00:02:07,770
So how did maple syrup production begin?

29
00:02:07,770 --> 00:02:13,160
Well, long before Europeans settled in these parts of the world,

30
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:17,080
the indigenous communities had started producing maple sugar.

31
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:20,752
They bored holes in the trunks of maple trees

32
00:02:20,752 --> 00:02:27,280
and used containers made of tree bark to collect the liquid sap as it poured out.

33
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:32,291
As they were unable to keep the liquid for any length of time -

34
00:02:32,291 --> 00:02:35,760
they didn't have storage facilities in those days -

35
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:43,470
they boiled the liquid by placing pieces of rock that had become scorching hot from the sun into the sap.

36
00:02:43,470 --> 00:02:47,120
They did this until it turned into sugar

37
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:51,800
and they were then able to use this to sweeten their food and drinks.

38
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:57,100
Since that time, improvements have been made to the process

39
00:02:57,120 --> 00:03:04,000
but it has changed very little overall.

40
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:09,300
So let's look at the production of maple syrup today.

41
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:16,710
Clearly, the maple forests are a valuable resource in many Canadian and North American communities.

42
00:03:16,710 --> 00:03:20,110
The trees have to be well looked after

43
00:03:20,110 --> 00:03:27,760
and they cannot be used to make syrup until the trunks reach a diameter of around 25 centimetres.

44
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:31,990
This can take anything up to 40 years.

45
00:03:31,990 --> 00:03:34,080
As I've already mentioned

46
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:40,010
maple trees need the right conditions to grow and also to produce sap.

47
00:03:40,010 --> 00:03:40,990
Why is this?

48
00:03:40,990 --> 00:03:45,040
Well, what happens is that during a cold night,

49
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:48,120
the tree absorbs water from the soil

50
00:03:48,120 --> 00:03:52,240
and that rises through the tree's vascular system.

51
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:54,372
But then in the warmer daytime,

52
00:03:54,372 --> 00:04:00,770
the change in temperature causes the water to be pushed back down to the bottom of the tree.

53
00:04:00,770 --> 00:04:04,900
This continual movement - up and down

54
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:09,790
leads to the formation of the sap needed for maple syrup production.

55
00:04:09,790 --> 00:04:13,552
When the tree is ready, it can be tapped

56
00:04:13,552 --> 00:04:18,132
and this involves drilling a small hole into the trunk

57
00:04:18,674 --> 00:04:22,330
and inserting a tube into it that ends in a bucket

58
00:04:22,330 --> 00:04:25,259
The trees can often take several taps,

59
00:04:25,259 --> 00:04:32,790
though the workers take care not to cause any damage to the healthy growth of the tree itself.

60
00:04:32,790 --> 00:04:38,760
The sap that comes out of the trees consists of 98 percent water

61
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:41,960
and 2 percent sugar and other nutrients.

62
00:04:41,960 --> 00:04:47,144
It has to be boiled so that much of that water evaporates,

63
00:04:47,144 --> 00:04:52,760
and this process has to take place immediately, using what are called evaporators.

64
00:04:52,760 --> 00:04:55,705
These are basically extremely large pans -

65
00:04:55,705 --> 00:05:01,686
the sap is poured into these, a fire is built

66
00:05:02,108 --> 00:05:05,520
and the pans are then heated until the sap boils

67
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:11,390
As it does this, the water evaporates, and the syrup begins to form.

68
00:05:11,390 --> 00:05:14,795
The evaporation process creates large quantities of steam,

69
00:05:14,795 --> 00:05:20,328
and the sap becomes thicker and denser, and, at just the right moment,

70
00:05:20,328 --> 00:05:25,700
when the sap is thick enough to be called maple syrup

71
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:27,990
the worker removes it from the heat.

72
00:05:27,990 --> 00:05:36,007
After this process, something called 'sugar sand' has to be filtered out as this builds up during the boiling

73
00:05:36,007 --> 00:05:41,070
and gives the syrup a cloudy appearance and a slightly gritty taste.

74
00:05:41,070 --> 00:05:42,772
Once this has been done,

75
00:05:42,772 --> 00:05:45,700
the syrup is ready to be packaged

76
00:05:45,700 --> 00:05:49,240
so that it can be used for a whole variety of products.

77
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:55,879
It takes 40 litres of sap to produce one litre of maple syrup

78
00:05:56,480 --> 00:05:59,990
so you can get an idea of how much is needed

79
00:05:59,990 --> 00:06:07,000
So that's the basic process. In places like Quebec where...
